CAT = "phrebDocumentation"; SEG = "DOC"
Q = [
("The first certificate of title issued after original registration of land under the Torrens system is the:",
 "Original Certificate of Title", ["Transfer Certificate of Title", "Condominium Certificate of Title", "Tax declaration"],
 "An OCT is issued on original registration under P.D. 1529. Every later transfer cancels the old title and issues a TCT in the new owner's name."),
("Under P.D. 1529, no title to registered land in derogation of the registered owner's can be acquired by:",
 "Prescription", ["Sale", "Inheritance", "Donation"],
 "Section 47 of P.D. 1529 provides that no title to registered land in derogation of the title of the registered owner shall be acquired by prescription or adverse possession."),
("A tax declaration in the seller's name proves:",
 "A claim of ownership, not title", ["Conclusive ownership", "A free and clear title", "That all real property taxes are paid in full"],
 "Tax declarations and receipts are not conclusive evidence of ownership. They support a claim of ownership, but a Torrens title is the best evidence of ownership of registered land."),
("A buyer checking a Torrens title for liens, adverse claims and lis pendens should get a certified true copy from the:",
 "Register of Deeds", ["Assessor's office", "Barangay hall", "BIR district office"],
 "The Registry of Deeds keeps the original title with all annotations. A certified true copy shows encumbrances that the owner's duplicate may not."),
("A notice that a case affecting a lot's title or possession is pending in court is called:",
 "Lis pendens", ["Adverse claim", "Caveat emptor", "Writ of possession"],
 "A notice of lis pendens under Section 76 of P.D. 1529 warns anyone dealing with the property that it is in litigation and that they take it subject to the result."),
("Under Section 70 of P.D. 1529, a registered adverse claim is effective for:",
 "Thirty days from registration", ["Seven days from registration", "One year from registration", "Until cancelled by the Register of Deeds"],
 "An adverse claim lasts thirty days from registration, after which it may be cancelled on a verified petition. Courts have held that it is not automatically lifted on the 31st day."),
("A deed of absolute sale of land must be a public instrument mainly because the law requires this for acts that:",
 "Create or transfer real rights", ["Involve movable property of any value", "Exceed one month", "Are signed abroad"],
 "Article 1358 requires acts and contracts that create, transmit, modify or extinguish real rights over immovable property to appear in a public instrument, which also makes them registrable."),
("Before the Register of Deeds will transfer a title after a sale, the BIR must issue a:",
 "Certificate Authorizing Registration", ["Mayor's permit", "Certificate of occupancy for the house", "Locational clearance"],
 "Section 58(E) of the Tax Code bars the RD from registering a transfer of real property until the BIR certifies that the transfer was reported and the tax paid, through the Certificate Authorizing Registration (eCAR)."),
("The agency that supervises the Registers of Deeds and keeps the national land titling records is the:",
 "Land Registration Authority", ["Bureau of Lands", "DHSUD", "National Housing Authority"],
 "The LRA, a unit of the Department of Justice under the Administrative Code of 1987, succeeded the Land Registration Commission of P.D. 1529 and supervises the Registries of Deeds."),
("An owner's duplicate title destroyed in a fire is restored through:",
 "Reconstitution or replacement", ["A new free patent", "A re-survey and a new tax declaration only", "Issuing a tax declaration"],
 "Section 109 of P.D. 1529 lets the court, on petition and after hearing, order a new owner's duplicate. A lost or destroyed original title in the Registry is reconstituted under R.A. 26."),
("The principle that a buyer may rely on what appears on the face of a Torrens title is the:",
 "Mirror principle", ["Curtain principle", "Insurance principle", "Doctrine of laches"],
 "Under the mirror principle the title reflects all facts about it, so a buyer in good faith need not look behind it, unless there are circumstances that should put him on inquiry."),
("Under P.D. 1529, a decree of registration becomes incontrovertible after:",
 "One year from entry", ["Thirty days from entry", "Five years from entry", "Ten years from entry"],
 "Section 32 allows a person deprived of land by fraud to petition for review within one year from entry of the decree; after that it becomes incontrovertible, leaving an action for damages."),
]
